@abstract
class_name GMDDefaultChannels
## Geometry Dash's per-object default colour channels.
##
## An object in a level string only carries key [code]21[/code] (base channel)
## and key [code]22[/code] (detail channel) when the creator changed them. When
## the keys are absent the object uses the default baked into the game's object
## definitions - [code]1004[/code] (Obj) for most bases, channel [code]1[/code]
## for most detail overlays, and a handful of literal channels such as
## [code]1010[/code] (Black) for pits, sawblades and the black block set.
##
## The importer used to treat a missing key as "no channel" and left the
## sprite tinted opaque white, which is what produced the random solid white
## blocks in imported levels: many Geometry Dash frames are pure white masks
## that only look right once tinted by their channel.
##
## Generated from the object definitions shipped with the open source gdrweb
## renderer (object IDs 1 to 1911). Only entries that differ from the common
## default are stored, so this table stays small. IDs outside it fall back to
## the defaults in [method base_channel] and [method detail_channel].

## The channel almost every base sprite uses when key 21 is absent.
const OBJ_CHANNEL: int = 1004

## The channel most detail overlays use when key 22 is absent.
const DEFAULT_DETAIL_CHANNEL: int = 1

## Objects whose default base channel isn't [constant OBJ_CHANNEL].
